--- A recursive function that answers nothing, with a bare `return`, whose LAST
--- statement is a call of itself, does not build compiled.
---
--- Interpreted (crow run -i): prints
---     3
---     2
---     1
--- Compiled (crow run): the program does not build:
---     error[E0069]: `return;` in a function whose return type is not `()`
---       --> src/main.rs:30:9
---     error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler refused what Crow
---     accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program (`crow run -i` runs it meanwhile)
--- Expected: the same output as interpreted.
---
--- `crow build --ir` shows `pure countdown(n: Int) -> ?` and the last statement
--- lowered as `return call countdown((n sub 1))`: the body's last expression is
--- taken as its answer, and that answer is the function's own, so the answer's type
--- is never settled; the emitter prints `-> String` for it, and the bare `return`
--- as `return;`. The same with two functions calling each other last (`ping` ends
--- `pong[n - 1]`, `pong` ends `ping[n - 1]`). It builds when the self-call is not the
--- last statement (`countdown[n - 1]` then `log[n]`), when the last statement calls
--- another function that answers nothing, and when the bare return is replaced by an
--- `if n gt 0` around the body. Not the fallible-function case fixed at 19:25
--- (findings/15_puzzle_game-1.co): nothing here can fail.
--- Found writing rosetta/avl_tree.co (drawing a tree: `draw[t.right]`, `log`,
--- `draw[t.left]`, after `if t eq 0` / `return`).

fn countdown[n]
    if n eq 0
        return
    log[n]
    countdown[n - 1]

countdown[3]
